# Ten laser shots. Rendered by tools/lasers.
bank   Lasers
clock  1512000
tick   220

category Lasers

sound Classic zap
audf  6 1 +6 16
audc  169 16 0 1
amp   0.45
note  A pure tone falling most of four octaves in 73ms at a volume that never moves. The shortest description of a laser there is, and the one to modify first: a bigger AUDF delta drops it further, a bigger Outer holds it longer.

sound Rapid pew
audf  10 1 +9 10
audc  168 10 0 1
amp   0.40
note  The same gesture in 45ms. Short enough to fire ten a second without the shots treading on each other, which is what a repeating weapon actually needs -- the design here is duration, not timbre.

sound Heavy blaster
audf  40 1 +7 26
audc  43 2 -1 12
amp   0.55
note  Starts well below the zap, on the 5-bit poly, and fades as it falls. Weight comes from starting low and taking 118ms about it; the poly repeats every 31 clocks, so the bottom of the sweep stays musical instead of hissing.

sound Charged beam
audf  120 1 -8 14
audf  16 1 +1 20
audc  170 14 0 1
audc  170 2 -1 11
amp   0.50
note  Two AUDF steps: a fast climb, then a slow settle back down while the volume fades out under it. The wind-up is what sells it as something charged rather than merely fired, and it costs one extra row in each chain.

sound Buzz cannon
audf  12 1 +10 18
audc  201 18 0 1
amp   0.50
note  The classic zap's sweep put through the 4-bit poly. That counter repeats every 15 clocks, so it has a pitch of its own underneath the sweep -- two pitches moving at once, which is the cheapest way to sound electrical.

sound Pulse rifle
audf  20 1 +2 18
audc  170 3 0 1
audc  160 3 0 1
audc  170 3 0 1
audc  160 3 0 1
audc  170 3 0 1
audc  160 3 0 1
amp   0.45
note  Three shots in 82ms, and the pitch chain knows nothing about it: AUDF falls smoothly the whole way while AUDC gates the volume on and off. The two chains run independently, so rhythm is AUDC's job and pitch is AUDF's.

sound Ion beam
audf  30 2 +5 30
audc  140 5 -1 12
amp   0.55
note  273ms of true noise sweeping down and fading -- the 17-bit poly, which does not repeat inside the life of the sound. Long and unpitched reads as a beam being held rather than a shot being fired.

sound Rasp
audf  18 1 +6 30
audc  163 1 -8 30
amp   0.45
wrap
note  Its AUDC delta of -8 flips the volume nibble's top bit every tick and wraps rather than clamping, so the level alternates between 3 and 11 at 110Hz -- that buzz *is* the sound, and it is a wrap being used on purpose. The arcade shooters of 1981 used exactly this trick.

sound Ricochet
audf  90 1 -6 13
audf  18 1 +9 14
audc  169 27 0 1
amp   0.45
note  Up then down, 123ms, volume flat throughout. An inverted V in pitch is what a shot glancing off something sounds like, and it needs no envelope work at all -- the whole shape is in the divider.

sound Siege cannon
audf  60 2 +9 22
audc  75 4 -1 11
amp   0.60
note  The bottom of the set: both short polys at once, falling for 200ms and fading. Inner 2 rather than 1 is what buys the length without twice the steps -- a step lasts Inner x Outer ticks.
